## 5. Feature Two — Find Object

### Intended outcome

A user requests a specific object, and OmniLens identifies whether that object is visible and communicates its supported position in the camera view.

### Required implementation

- Accept the target through an accessible text field and voice input if supported.
- Validate empty, invalid, ambiguous, and unsupported requests.
- Normalize spelling and resolve reasonable synonyms without changing the user's intended target.
- Capture a fresh frame and send it to the actual detector.
- Verify that the detector supports the requested category.
- Return genuine detections, bounding boxes, confidence scores, and image-relative positions where supported.
- Handle multiple instances of the same object.
- Provide an honest result when the target is not detected.
- Distinguish a successful search with no matching detection from a model or network failure.

### Object coverage

Evaluate the current model's real capabilities instead of assuming that it recognizes every object.

If a closed-set model such as a standard COCO-trained YOLO model cannot recognize important requested objects, investigate suitable alternatives such as:

- Open-vocabulary object detection.
- Text-conditioned object detection.
- Vision-language models.
- A hybrid detector and vision-model pipeline.

Compare these options using the same evaluation examples. Consider accuracy, class coverage, localization quality, latency, memory, privacy, cost, licensing, and deployment feasibility.

Potential test targets should include ordinary objects, doors, door handles, windows, window grills, curtains, fans, glasses, packaged food, and other objects relevant to the intended users. Include human activities or gestures only where a suitable, validated model supports them.

### Spatial guidance

Use image geometry to report positions such as:

- “The object appears on your left.”
- “The object is near the centre of the camera view.”
- “The object appears on your right.”

Use more detailed horizontal or vertical zones only when they are consistently defined and validated.

Do not convert bounding-box size into an unsupported exact distance. Do not tell the user to reach, grab, or walk toward an object solely because the detector identifies it.

### Testing

Create a labelled test set containing supported and unsupported objects, multiple instances, partial occlusion, different lighting, different distances, and visually similar objects.

Measure:

- Precision and recall.
- False positives and missed detections.
- Localization quality.
- Per-class performance.
- End-to-end successful searches.
- Latency and failure rates.

Optimize for the intended use case, not just a high aggregate benchmark score.

