## 7. Feature Four — SafePath Camera-Based Obstacle Awareness

### Intended outcome

SafePath uses the live camera and validated computer-vision capabilities to alert the user to potentially relevant visible obstacles.

This is an assistive awareness feature, not a certified collision-avoidance system.

### Required implementation

- Trace the active camera-to-inference pipeline.
- Identify the actual model, supported classes, and preprocessing implementation.
- Ensure inference operates on recent, valid frames.
- Fix overlapping requests, stale results, tracking failures, and duplicate alerts.
- Evaluate relevant obstacles, including people, furniture, bags, poles, and other supported categories.
- Investigate suitable improvements to object detection and scene analysis.
- Test OpenCV brightness correction, CLAHE, denoising, resizing, and other preprocessing only when appropriate.
- Compare changes against the original baseline using the same labelled test set.
- Stabilize repeated detections when tracking is supported.
- Avoid excessive speech and alert flickering.
- Provide accessible Pause, Resume, Repeat, Stop, and Stop Speaking controls.

### Alert behaviour

Use short, cautious messages when the visual evidence supports them.

Examples:

- “A possible obstacle is visible on your left.”
- “A person is visible near the centre of the camera view.”
- “I cannot reliably assess the current view. Please use your established mobility method.”

Do not claim an object is dangerously close, predict a collision, or assert that a crossing is safe without a separately validated capability.

Most importantly:

**An empty detection list does not prove that the path is clear. A failed camera, unavailable model, invalid frame, timeout, or failed inference must never be interpreted as a successful obstacle assessment.**

Distinguish explicitly between:

- Potential obstacle detected.
- No obstacle detected by the current system in this frame.
- Camera or image quality insufficient.
- Model unavailable.
- Analysis failed.
- Feature paused or stopped.

Do not announce “path clear” as a guarantee.

### Evaluation

Use labelled scenes in controlled conditions to measure missed obstacles, false alerts, latency, temporal stability, and performance under different lighting and occlusion conditions.

Test the complete spoken-alert pipeline, not only the detector.

Never instruct users to rely on OmniLens instead of a cane, guide dog, orientation-and-mobility training, or established mobility practices. Do not encourage uncontrolled testing near roads, stairs, traffic, or other hazards.

